  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\n`proto2num()` (line 431–447) consumes only one leading modifier character via a `switch` statement. The documentation says entries may be \"prefixed by zero or more modifiers.\" Before commit `86ac471d`, a loop consumed all modifiers. The replacement switch reads only the first character.\n\n`--proto '+-ftp'` reads `+` (action=allow), then passes `-ftp` to `proto_token()` (line 466). `-ftp` is not a recognized protocol name → warning emitted, FTP stays in the default set. `--silent` suppresses the warning entirely.\n\nThe same parser handles `--proto-redir`, so redirect protocol restrictions are equally bypassable.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```sh\npython3 poc_proto_bypass.py <sha>\n```\n\n```sh\n# Denied correctly:\ncurl --proto '-file' file:///etc/hostname    # exit 1 (protocol denied)\n\n# Bypass:\ncurl --proto '+-file' file:///etc/hostname   # exit 0 — reads the file!\ncurl --silent --proto '+-file' file:///etc/hostname  # no warning shown\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\n```c\n// src/tool_paramhlp.c:431-447\nswitch(*str++) {        // ← consumes ONE character only\ncase '=': action = set;   break;\ncase '-': action = deny;  break;\ncase '+': action = allow; break;\ndefault:  str--; plen++;  break;\n}\n// str now points to \"-ftp\", which proto_token() doesn't recognize\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nA trusted wrapper or script that uses the documented stacked-modifier syntax (e.g. `--proto '+-ftp'` intending to deny FTP after first allowing all) silently leaves the denied protocol enabled. An untrusted party who controls the URL or a redirect target can then reach a protocol the wrapper explicitly tried to block. `--proto-redir` is equally affected, so a server-controlled 3xx redirect can escalate to `file://`, `gopher://`, or `ftp://` reads despite the wrapper's restrictions. `--silent` suppresses the only visible indicator (the \"unrecognized protocol\" warning). Direct libcurl API callers are not affected — the bug is in the CLI argument parser only.",
